<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experimental results aim to explore the design space of vectorized FlashAttention and identify the set of parameters that yield the best performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are conducted using a fully implemented decoupled vector unit integrated with an out-of-order superscalar processor, corresponding to model 1bDV in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ta et al. (2022)</a></cite>. The use of a high-performance superscalar core alongside the vector engine is intended to ensure that the scalar portion of the processor does not become a limiting factor in evaluating vector engine performance. Essentially, this configuration allows the vector unit’s performance to be measured in isolation, free from interference or bottlenecks introduced by the scalar side. The system is modeled using the gem5 simulator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Binkert et al. (2011)</a></cite>, and the key architectural parameters of the simulated processor are listed in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Operating in parallel on multiple queries ‣ 4 Tiling in Vectorized FlashAttention ‣ Vectorized FlashAttention with Low-cost Exponential Computation in RISC-V Vector Processors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg6" title="Algorithm 6 ‣ 4 Tiling in Vectorized FlashAttention ‣ Vectorized FlashAttention with Low-cost Exponential Computation in RISC-V Vector Processors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> was executed on attention layers of state-of-the-art LLM architectures available on HuggingFace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wolf et al. (2020)</a></cite>. Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5 Experimental results ‣ Vectorized FlashAttention with Low-cost Exponential Computation in RISC-V Vector Processors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the size of the hidden dimension, number of attention heads, and per-head dimensionality for the first attention layer of each model. In transformer architectures, the hidden size refers to the dimensionality of token embeddings and internal hidden states—that is, the number of features used to represent each token at any layer. A larger hidden size enables the model to learn and represent more complex and nuanced patterns. Conversely, the number of attention heads defines how many parallel attention mechanisms are applied to each token. Each head processes information in a distinct subspace of size <span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">hidden size/num heads</span>, known as the head dimension, which serves as the primary focus of our evaluations. Notably, the head dimension remains consistent across all layers within each LLM.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>The characteristics of contemporary LLM models available on HuggingFace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wolf et al. (2020)</a></cite></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
